{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Weight: W = mg. Mass (kg) is not the same as weight (N); g = 10 N/kg on Earth",
   "Work done: W = Fd. Distance must be in the direction of the force",
   "Hooke's law: F = ke. Extension e must be in metres (not cm)",
   "Elastic PE: E_e = ½ke². Extension is SQUARED — doubling extension quadruples stored energy"
  ],
  "title": "Forces, Work Done and Elasticity",
  "content": "<p>Four equations that are commonly mixed up — learn the right one for the right context.</p><p><strong>Weight:</strong> W = mg. This is a force (N), not a mass (kg). Mass is constant; weight changes with gravitational field strength.</p><p><strong>Work done:</strong> W = Fd. The distance must be in the direction of the force (J = N m).</p><p><strong>Hooke's law:</strong> F = ke, where k is the spring constant (N/m) and e is extension (m). Only valid up to the limit of proportionality — beyond that, plastic deformation occurs.</p><p><strong>Elastic PE:</strong> \\(E_e = \\tfrac{1}{2}ke^2\\). Extension is squared — just like KE.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Confusing mass (kg) and weight (N)</li><li>Using extension in cm instead of m in F = ke and E_e = ½ke²</li><li>Forgetting to square extension in the elastic PE equation</li></ul>"
 },
 "topic_links": {
  "prerequisites": []
 },
 "exam_context": {
